        "Attributes": [
          {
            "Id": 758266,
            "attributeType": "LongString",
            "attributeName": "AIDescription",
            "attributeRelationType": "None",
            "Value": "The ColecoVision, released in August 1982, was a groundbreaking home video game console known for its impressive arcade-quality graphics and innovative features. Developed by Coleco Industries, it distinguished itself by being able to play Atari 2600 games through an expansion module, broadening its game library significantly. Bundled with the immensely popular *Donkey Kong*, the ColecoVision quickly gained traction, selling over 500,000 units by the end of 1982 and eventually exceeding one million. \n\nPowered by a Zilog Z80A processor and a Texas Instruments TMS9918 video chip, it offered a 256x192 pixel resolution and 16 colors. Other notable titles included *Donkey Kong Junior*, *Carnival*, and *Zaxxon*. Despite its initial success and contribution to the early multi-platform console movement, the ColecoVision was discontinued in 1984 due to the video game market crash."
          },
